# A reference file, not a file to copy blindly.
#
# On a VPS that serves nothing else, this can become /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
# outright. On a machine that already has one — another site, another
# service — **take the `import` line only** and add it to the existing file.
# `sudo cp deploy/Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile` would delete whatever was
# already there, and Caddy is a single-config-file server: there is only one
# of them, and everything on the machine shares it.
#
# The site block itself is no longer here. It lives in deploy/fernscout.caddy,
# which this file imports and which an existing Caddyfile can import in
# exactly the same way — one line, added once, pointing into the checkout. A
# proxy directive added in a release then reaches the machine with the commit
# that made it, because `git pull` updates the file Caddy is reading. Before
# that, a change here reached a merged Caddyfile only if somebody remembered:
# B01's `header_up X-Forwarded-For` was deployed, reported healthy, and had no
# effect at all until it was applied by hand a day later. That is B66.
#
# Automatic HTTPS: Caddy requests and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate for
# {$CADDY_DOMAIN} on first request, with no cron job and no certbot. Needs
# :80 reachable for the ACME HTTP-01 challenge and :443 for the site itself —
# Caddy binds both itself; nothing else may hold :80 or :443.
#
# {$CADDY_DOMAIN} / {$CADDY_ACME_EMAIL} come from the environment (.env);
# see .env.example. For a bare local/offline test, run with
# CADDY_DOMAIN=:80 to skip TLS entirely (see docs/runbook.md) — Caddy detects
# the plain :80/:8080-style address and serves HTTP-only automatically.
#
# The global options block (rather than a per-site `tls <email>`) keeps this
# working for any CADDY_DOMAIN, TLS or not. `email` needs a non-empty
# argument — set a real address here before the first certificate request
# so this never sees a blank one, even before .env exists.
#
# Merging into an existing Caddyfile: **do not bring this block with you.** A
# second global options block is a config error, and adding one where there
# was none changes ACME behaviour for the sites already being served. The
# import below is the only line to take.
{
	email {$CADDY_ACME_EMAIL}
}

# Adjust the path if the checkout is not at /srv/fernscout. Caddy reads it as
# root, and it must stay readable to root — an import Caddy cannot open is a
# config error and the reload is refused, which is the loud failure and the
# right one.
import /srv/fernscout/deploy/fernscout.caddy
